  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and very humid conditions are expected to \npersist through Tuesday of next week. A Heat Advisory is in effect \nfor portions of the area through Saturday.\n\nA relatively stagnant synoptic pattern is developing across our \nregion. Quasi zonal mid and upper level flow will be in place \nthrough the middle of next week as Bermuda high pressure persists \noffshore. This will keep a steady stream of very warm and moist air \nin place across the region through the period.\n\nA prolonged period of hot and, most notably, very humid conditions \nis expected to remain in place for the next several days. We look to \nhave a weak cold frontal passage Sunday, ushering in slightly drier \nair. This will give some slight reprieve from the excessive \nhumidity, though highs will still be in the upper 80s to low 90s. \nMoisture advection will return again by Monday though, and hot and \nvery humid conditions are anticipated to return Monday through \nTuesday. A somewhat stronger cold front looks to come through late \nTuesday or Wednesday, which will help break the excessive heat and \nhumidity some.\n\nA Heat Advisory remains in effect for portions of eastern \nPennsylvania, much of New Jersey except the far southern counties, \nas well as New Castle County, Delware. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...There are daily chances for showers and \nthunderstorms through Saturday, as well as portions of next \nweek. There is at least a marginal risk each day for localized \nheavy rainfall leading to flash flooding and locally damaging \nwind gusts with any stronger thunderstorms that develop.\n\nA Severe Thunderstorm Watch remains in effect for the Delmarva\nand most of southern New Jersey through 9 PM. For areas outside\nof these areas, the watch has been cancelled. MLCAPE this \nafternoon has reached near or over 3000 J/kg with some favorable\nlapse rates. However, shear remains around 20-30 kt. The \nprimary risk is damaging wind gusts with any severe \nthunderstorms. There remains a Slight (level 2 out of 5) risk \nfor areas near and northwest of the I-95 corridor. The remainder\nof the area is under a Marginal (level 1 out of 5) risk for \nsevere thunderstorms. Another concern is the heavy rainfall \npotential. There is a Slight (level 2 out of 4) for excessive \nrainfall for areas near and NW of I-95 corridor. The remainder \nof the area is under a Marginal (level 1 out of 4) risk for \nexcessive rainfall. PWATs of 2 inches or greater today along \nwith a warm cloud layer process showcases the heavy rainfall \nrisk. Given these conditions, rainfall rates of 2-3 inches per \nhour are possible.\n\nOn Saturday, the extent of the convection remains more unclear. The \ncurrent guidance shows isolated convection developing during the \nlater afternoon into the evening hours. PWATs look slightly lower \nthan today, and mean flow is a bit more westerly than southwesterly, \nbut overall the thermodynamics appear similar. There will be less \nforcing, so the coverage is more isolated compared to today. We \nremain in a Marginal (level 1 out of 4) risk for excessive rainfall \nfrom WPC. In terms of the severe weather potential, SPC has added a \nMarginal (level 1 out of 5) risk for isolated severe thunderstorms \nfor most of our area with the primary hazard being damaging wind \ngusts. \n\nWe look to have a weak cold frontal passage by Sunday, ushering in \nslightly drier air. This combined with subtle subsidence aloft will \nhelp briefly limit convection potential on Sunday. Moisture and \nforcing aloft will return by Monday as mean flow shifts more \nwesterly and mid level flow increases. This should result in a \ngreater severe thunderstorm threat compared to this week, and \nperhaps slightly lower flash flooding threat, but the details into \nthe period early next week remain unclear. Long story short, Monday \nand Tuesday look to continue the hot and humid conditions and daily \nthunderstorm chances.\n\nA somewhat stronger cold front looks to come through late Tuesday or \nWednesday, which will help break the excessive heat and humidity \nsome, but the pattern aloft may still support diurnally driven \nconvection.",
